---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { TRANSFORMS } from '../../../index/normalize.ts';
import { PROJECTION, project } from '../../../index/model.ts';
import { REPO_URL, fileUrl, latest, weights, weightsPath } from '../lib/data.ts';
import SourceCite from '../components/SourceCite.tsx';
import { DISCOUNTS } from '../../../sources/corporate.ts';
import { POLL_HALF_LIFE_DAYS, POLL_MAX_AGE_DAYS } from '../../../sources/polling.ts';

const subKeys = Object.keys(weights.subIndices);
const metricsOf = (k: string) => Object.entries(weights.metrics).filter(([, s]) => s === k).map(([id]) => id);
const unscored = Object.entries(weights.metrics).filter(([, s]) => s === null).map(([id]) => id);
const anchor = (t: (typeof TRANSFORMS)[string]) =>
  t.kind === 'linear' ? `${t.at0} → ${t.at100}` : `log, 0 → ${t.at100.toExponential(0)}`;
const fixture = project(34, 88, '2026-10-14');
---
<Base title="Method: The Trigger" description="How the readiness index, normalization and projection are computed, and where they are weak." page="method">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1 style="margin-top:24px;font-size:20px">Method</h1>
    <p class="muted">
      Everything on the tracker is computed by pure functions from committed data. This page is generated from the
      same code and weights file that produce the score, so it cannot drift from what the site shows.
      Current snapshot: <a href={fileUrl(`data/${latest.date}.json`)}>{latest.date}</a>, weights <code>{weights.version}</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>Pipeline</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>A GitHub Action runs once a day. Each source module fetches its upstream records and returns raw values, each with a deep link to the record it came from.</li>
      <li>Values are normalized to 0–100, where higher always means closer to a dividend being paid.</li>
      <li>Normalized metrics are averaged into six sub-indices, which are weighted into one composite.</li>
      <li>The composite drives the projection. The whole result is committed as <code>data/YYYY-MM-DD.json</code>. Git history is the time series; past files are never rewritten. Corrections land as a new commit with a higher <code>revision</code>.</li>
      <li>The site is a static build of those files. No number is fetched in your browser.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 id="weights">Weighting</h2>
    <p>
      The weights are an editorial judgment, kept in <a href={fileUrl(weightsPath)}>{weightsPath}</a> with a
      comment on each line. Published versions are never edited; v1 stays in the repo and still runs. The model contains no weights of its own. To argue with them, copy the file to a new version,
      change it, run <code>pnpm snapshot</code>, and open a pull request with the score your weights produce.
    </p>
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Sub-index</th><th>Weight</th><th>Now</th><th>Inputs (equal weight within)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {subKeys.map((k) => (
          <tr id={k}>
            <td>{weights.subIndices[k]?.label}</td>
            <td class="num">{weights.subIndices[k]?.weight.toFixed(2)}</td>
            <td class="num">{latest.subIndices[k]?.toFixed(1) ?? 'no data'}</td>
            <td class="small"><code>{metricsOf(k).join(', ')}</code></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:8px">
      Composite = Σ weight × sub-index. A sub-index is the mean of its normalized metrics. If a sub-index has no data in a
      run, it is dropped and the remaining weights are rescaled to sum to 1; the snapshot records the share of weight
      that was fed (<code>weightCovered</code>, now {Math.round(latest.weightCovered * 100)}%).
    </p>

    <h3 id="changelog">Weights changelog: v1 to v2</h3>
    <p>The only reweighting so far. Same inputs scored <span class="num">25.7</span> under v1 <SourceCite href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/80ca01b/data/2026-10-14.json`} name="r2, v1" /> and <span class="num">28.1</span> under v2 <SourceCite href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/8326a8a/data/2026-10-14.json`} name="r3, v2" />. Every version, every revision of the first snapshot, and the rationale are in <a href={fileUrl('WEIGHTS.md')}>WEIGHTS.md</a>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Fiscal pressure 0.15 → 0.05.</b> The deficit says whether the government is stretched, not whether AI is displacing anyone, and its sign is ambiguous. In v1 it carried as much weight as public opinion.</li>
      <li><b>Labor dislocation 0.25 → 0.30.</b> It is the thesis.</li>
      <li><b>Corporate equity 0.10 → 0.15, and it now scores the pipeline.</b> v1 counted binding commitments only against a $100B anchor, which pinned the sub-index at 0 while the largest live proposal in the data sat unscored.</li>
      <li><b>Public opinion averages several polls</b> instead of reading the latest one. Same weight, better input.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="polls">Poll average</h3>
    <p>
      Each national poll in <a href={fileUrl('sources/curated/polling.yaml')}>polling.yaml</a> gets weight
      √n × 0.5<sup>age / {POLL_HALF_LIFE_DAYS} days</sup>, halved again if the sponsor or pollster leans left or right.
      Polls older than {Math.round(POLL_MAX_AGE_DAYS / 365)} years drop out. √n because sampling error shrinks with √n.
      Partisan polls count half rather than zero: they are still data, and a left and a right poll of equal size
      offset each other's lean.
    </p>

    <h3 id="pipeline">Equity pipeline</h3>
    <p>
      Every proposal for AI firms to put equity or cash into a public fund is listed in
      <a href={fileUrl('sources/curated/corporate.yaml')}>corporate.yaml</a> with a status, and counts at a discount:
    </p>
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Status</th><th>Counts at</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>binding</td><td class="num">×{DISCOUNTS.binding}</td><td>Legally enforceable and dated.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>signed</td><td class="num">×{DISCOUNTS.signed}</td><td>Signed letter or term sheet, not yet enforceable.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>reported</td><td class="num">×{DISCOUNTS.reported}</td><td>Talks reported by named outlets.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>floated</td><td class="num">×{DISCOUNTS.floated}</td><td>Op-ed, interview, or proposal with no counterparty.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:8px">
      A proposal with no reported size is listed and counts $0. The binding-only total is still recorded, unscored.
    </p>

    <h3 id="gap">The gap</h3>
    <p>
      Labor dislocation minus political momentum. Positive means pain is outrunning the response, the condition the
      thesis says forces checks. Negative means elites are front-running the crisis: bills, executive orders and equity
      proposals are arriving before the job losses show up in the labor data. Neither is good or bad on its own; the
      direction of travel is the signal.
    </p>

    <h2 id="normalization">Normalization</h2>
    <p>
      Every raw value is mapped to 0–100 with a linear transform clamped at two anchors: the value that means
      "nothing is happening" (0) and the value that means "the trigger has been pulled" (100). Dollar and recipient
      totals use a log scale, because the first $100M of precedent matters far more than the next. Anchors live in
      <a href={fileUrl('index/normalize.ts')}>index/normalize.ts</a>, each with its reasoning.
    </p>
    <table class="table">
      <thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Anchors (0 → 100)</th><th>Unit</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(TRANSFORMS).map(([id, t]) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="small"><code>{id}</code></td>
            <td class="num nowrap">{anchor(t)}</td>
            <td class="small nowrap">{t.unit}</td>
            <td class="small">{t.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="small muted" style="margin-top:8px">
      Unscored, recorded for calibration only: <code>{unscored.join(', ')}</code>. Prediction markets and GovTrack's
      prognosis are models of their own; we compare against them rather than stack them into ours.
    </p>

    <h2 id="projection">Projection</h2>
    <pre>{`projectedYear = ${PROJECTION.anchorYear} − (readiness / 100) × ${PROJECTION.spanYears}    clamp [${PROJECTION.minYear}, ${PROJECTION.maxYear}]
confidence    = clamp(0.5 × readiness + 0.5 × sourceCoverage%, ${PROJECTION.confidenceMin}, ${PROJECTION.confidenceMax})
bandMonths    = (100 − readiness) / 100 × ${PROJECTION.maxBandMonths}`}</pre>
    <p>
      A readiness of 0 projects to the second quarter of {Math.floor(PROJECTION.anchorYear)}; a readiness of 100 projects
      to the floor. The projection never lands before the snapshot it belongs to. Confidence blends how far along the
      index is with how many sources answered today, and is capped at {PROJECTION.confidenceMax}% because no index of this kind
      deserves more. The band widens as readiness falls. The site never shows the date without the band.
    </p>
    <p>
      Regression fixture, enforced by the test suite: readiness 34 with 88% of sources live gives
      <span class="num">{fixture.projectedDate}</span> (Q3 2030), <span class="num">{Math.round(fixture.confidence * 100)}%</span> confidence,
      ±<span class="num">{fixture.bandMonths}</span> months. Today: readiness <span class="num">{latest.readiness}</span>,
      coverage <span class="num">{latest.sourceCoveragePct}%</span> → <span class="num">{latest.projectedDate}</span>,
      <span class="num">{Math.round(latest.confidence * 100)}%</span>, ±<span class="num">{latest.bandMonths}</span> months.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sources">Sources and failure handling</h2>
    <p>
      Tier 1 (BLS, Congress.gov, FRED, Federal Register) are federal or open APIs. Tier 2 and 3 include SEC
      EDGAR, LegiScan, prediction markets, and hand-curated files for publishers with no API (Challenger layoff reports,
      national polls, pilot program records). Curated files live in <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/sources/curated`}>sources/curated/</a>,
      every value with a deep link, and a curated file is treated as a failed source if no one has re-reviewed it
      within its stated window.
    </p>
    <p>
      When a source fails, its last known values are carried into the new snapshot marked <code>stale</code> (dashed
      card on the tracker), the failure is listed in <code>sourcesFailed</code> with its error, and confidence drops through
      the coverage term. A source that needs an API key nobody configured is skipped, and does not count against coverage.
      Terms for each source are in <a href={fileUrl('DATA_LICENSE.md')}>DATA_LICENSE.md</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="predictions">Grading ourselves</h2>
    <p>
      Every projection is recorded in <a href={fileUrl('predictions/ledger.json')}>predictions/ledger.json</a> with the date it
      was made and its confidence, and is later resolved as a hit or a miss. <a href={fileUrl('SCOREBOARD.md')}>SCOREBOARD.md</a> is
      regenerated from the ledger on every run, including our Brier score once anything resolves. Entries are append-only.
    </p>

    <h2 id="weaknesses">Known weaknesses</h2>
    <p>We would rather you read this list than discover it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The projection is a line, not a model.</b> It maps one number to one date with constants we chose. It has no causal structure, no fitted parameters, and no backtest, because no country has paid an AI dividend to fit against. Treat the date as a summary of the index, not a forecast with error bars from data.</li>
      <li><b>The weights and anchors are opinions.</b> Every one is defensible and none is derived. Small changes move the date by years. That is why they live in a file you can fork.</li>
      <li><b>Confidence is not a probability.</b> It is a formula over readiness and data coverage, clamped to 35–80%. It has not been calibrated against outcomes, and the scoreboard will take years to say anything.</li>
      <li><b>Entry-level postings are proxied.</b> No free federal series isolates entry-level white-collar hiring, so v1 uses JOLTS openings in professional and business services, which includes senior roles and non-AI-exposed work.</li>
      <li><b>AI-attributed layoffs depend on what employers say.</b> Challenger counts cuts where the employer cited AI. Companies have reasons to over- or under-claim AI. The trailing-12-month figure is our arithmetic across Challenger's monthly reports, not a figure Challenger publishes.</li>
      <li><b>Executive action is counted bluntly.</b> The Federal Register metric counts presidential documents whose text mentions artificial intelligence. Some are about AI; some mention it in passing. It measures attention, not direction.</li>
      <li><b>Bill tracking depends on a watchlist.</b> Congress.gov has no reliable full-text bill search in its API, so we follow a curated list. A relevant bill we have not listed is invisible until someone adds it.</li>
      <li><b>Some precedent figures are unverified.</b> Cumulative guaranteed-income dollars and recipients are maintainer figures; the pilot dashboard does not publish a total we could confirm. They are flagged in the data and on the card.</li>
      <li><b>Polling is thin.</b> National UBI polls are rare and question wording varies widely: "a monthly payment to every adult that would require significant government funding" and "a monthly stimulus check to every American" are not the same question. The average weights by sample size and recency, not by wording, and two of the current polls have partisan sponsors.</li>
      <li><b>Proposal sizes are our arithmetic.</b> "5% of OpenAI" is converted to dollars at OpenAI's last disclosed valuation. A private valuation is a negotiated number, and the discounts by status are an editorial choice.</li>
      <li><b>Headcount comes from prose.</b> 10-Ks rarely tag employee counts in XBRL, so EDGAR headcount is parsed from the Human Capital paragraph. A reworded sentence can drop a company from the comparison, and headcount is global while the thesis is about US payroll.</li>
      <li><b>Fiscal pressure cuts both ways.</b> High deficits can block new spending as easily as force it. We score deficits as pressure toward change, at a weight of 0.05 in v2; you may reasonably flip the sign.</li>
      <li><b>GovTrack is retired.</b> govtrack.us returned 502 on every request from the first snapshot, so the source was removed on 2026-10-15. Its prognosis was unscored, so the index did not move; Congress.gov supplies bill status.</li>
      <li><b>Renormalizing over missing sub-indices hides gaps.</b> If a whole sub-index goes dark, the composite leans on what remains. <code>weightCovered</code> exposes this, but the headline number does not.</li>
      <li><b>Mixed cadences.</b> Inputs update daily, monthly and annually (the deficit is a fiscal-year figure). A daily snapshot is mostly yesterday's numbers.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Out of scope for v0.1</h2>
    <p class="muted">Accounts, a database, a public API, i18n, mobile apps, alerts and a state-level map. See the roadmap in the README.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
